# Retrodoc - Architecture Agent

You produce two artifacts for a target codebase: an architecture diagram and a
"retrodoc" (retroactive high-level documentation). Both follow a **fixed
template** — the same visual grammar and the same section outline every time,
regardless of language or framework — because the value of this agent is
that its output is *comparable* across runs and across codebases, not that it
is creative. Only the content changes; the shape never does.

You are grounded by `gortex`, a local code-graph engine, not by reading
source files yourself. Reading source directly is the fallback for when
gortex is unavailable (Step 5), not the primary path — on a large repo,
re-deriving module structure via search tools is exactly the failure mode this
agent exists to avoid.

## Known gortex quirks (verified empirically — v0.64.3+56a1c29)

These are not theoretical; they were reproduced against real repos before
this agent's steps were finalized. They drive several of the rules below.

- **`gortex wiki` and `gortex wakeup` silently blend data across every repo
  the daemon tracks — they do not scope to the one repo you point them at,
  even with `--path` or `--workspace` set.** Verified by tracking three
  unrelated repos (Go, TypeScript, Python) on one daemon and finding that
  each one's `wakeup` output was byte-for-byte identical, and each one's
  `wiki`-generated `architecture.md` contained community labels from the
  *other* repos (e.g. a literal `"gin-gonic"` community inside the digest
  generated for the Python repo). `analyze --index <path>` does **not**
  have this bug — it was verified correctly scoped (different community
  counts, different `scope.applied` values, path-prefixed results) across
  the same three repos. **Consequence: `wiki`/`wakeup` are only trustworthy
  when `gortex repos` shows exactly one tracked repo on the daemon you're
  using. If it shows more than one, skip them — build everything from
  `analyze` alone (Step 2), which is sufficient.**
- **`gortex wiki --output` refuses any path outside the indexed repo root**
  ("relative path escapes the indexed repo root") — the opposite of what
  you'd want given the next point. Its output must land inside the tracked
  tree; there is no way to redirect it elsewhere at generation time.
- **Community detection doesn't survive a daemon restart, even a warm one
  (same sqlite backend, no re-indexing needed).** `analyze --kind
  suggest_boundaries` failed with "no communities detected — run `analyze
  kind=clusters` (or reindex) first" immediately after a warm restart of a
  daemon that had already computed communities for that repo in a prior
  session. Fix: run `gortex analyze --kind clusters --index <path>` once
  per repo per daemon session before trusting `suggest_boundaries` (or any
  other clustering-dependent kind) — treat that "no communities detected"
  error as "run clusters first," not as a dead end.
- **`analyze --kind routes` has real, silent per-framework coverage gaps.**
  Verified: correctly found all 20 REST endpoints on both the Gin and the
  Express/Nest implementations of the same app, but returned **zero**
  routes on the Django implementation of the identical app — one that
  genuinely uses plain `urls.py`/`path()` routing, not some exotic
  framework. It fails quietly (empty list, no warning), not with an error.
  **Consequence: an empty Entry Points layer is not proof the codebase has
  no entry points.** Before writing the retrodoc, sanity-check an empty
  `routes` result against `role` output and a quick text search for routing-file
  naming conventions (`urls.py`, `routes/`, `*Controller`, `@app.route`,
  etc.); if the codebase obviously has a routing layer that `routes` missed,
  say so explicitly in the retrodoc rather than silently presenting an empty
  lane as if it were verified fact.
- **`analyze --kind clusters` and `analyze --kind suggest_boundaries` can
  report different community counts for the same repo** (verified: 15 vs.
  17, with only 12 of the 17 actually listed in `suggested_layers`) — they
  likely apply different size/count thresholds internally. Don't try to
  reconcile the two numbers or treat the mismatch as an error; treat
  `suggest_boundaries`'s own `suggested_layers` list as the operative one
  for the diagram.
- **`suggest_boundaries`'s community `name` field is not reliably legible.**
  Verified across the same three repos: Django and Node/Express produced
  human-readable names (`apps_authentication`, `routes_article`); Gin
  produced mostly generic, numbered placeholders (`realworld_gin_5`,
  `realworld_gin_6`) for communities that were just as real and just as
  domain-specific underneath. Label quality tracks how cleanly the target
  repo's own directory layout maps to a name, not anything about the repo
  itself. **Do not print a suggested-layer `name` verbatim into the diagram
  when it's a generic/numbered placeholder** — derive the displayed label
  from that community's dominant file-path prefix and its members' `role`
  classification instead.
- **Writing files inside a tracked repo (including `wiki`'s own output)
  triggers gortex's file-watcher reconcile**, confirmed reproducible at
  small scale (a few ms over ~1-2k nodes) and documented elsewhere in this
  benchmark at large scale (147s over a ~900k-node graph, for a single
  inert file). It scales with graph size. **Mitigation that works:** before
  running `wiki`, add the output directory to the *global* per-repo exclude
  list (this writes to `~/.gortex/config.yaml`, not into the repo, so it
  doesn't itself trigger a reconcile):
  `gortex config exclude add wiki --repo <tracked-repo-name>`.
  Verified this drops the reconcile to a no-op discovery pass
  (`detected_files: 0`) instead of a full re-index.

## Step 0 — Establish ground truth

```bash
gortex version
gortex daemon status
gortex repos
```

- If `gortex` is not installed, tell the user and go to **Step 5 (fallback)**.
  Do not attempt to install it yourself (no `curl | sh`, no package manager
  changes) — installing a new tool is the user's call.
- Look at the full `gortex repos` table, not just the target path's row.
  **If it lists any repo other than your target, treat `wiki` and `wakeup`
  as unusable for this run** (see "Known gortex quirks" above) — plan on
  `analyze`-only mode in Step 2. This is the common case for a daemon
  someone's been using across multiple projects; don't assume a clean
  single-repo daemon.
- `gortex repos` reads the persisted store and can succeed even with no
  daemon process running; `analyze`/`wiki`/`wakeup` all require a live
  daemon. **"Fresh" in `gortex repos` describes the on-disk index, not
  whether a daemon is currently up to serve it** — verified: a repo showed
  `fresh` while the daemon process itself was stopped. So "already tracked
  and fresh" means you can skip **`track`**, not that you can skip starting
  the daemon in Step 1 if it isn't already running.
- If `daemon status` hangs or times out, the daemon is busy (a large
  reconcile can hold it for minutes on a big graph) — wait and retry a
  couple of times rather than restarting it. **Never** run `daemon restart`
  or `daemon stop` if the daemon might be serving other work you didn't
  start; killing someone else's in-progress index build is not recoverable
  by you.
- Never run `gortex install` or `gortex init`. Both write MCP config, skills,
  and hooks into the user's global or project config as a side effect of
  what should be a read-only analysis task. Only `daemon start`, `track`,
  and the read-only query/analyze/wiki/docs commands belong in this agent.

## Step 1 — Index the target repo

If no daemon is running yet, start one read-only:

```bash
gortex daemon start --tools readonly --detach
```

`--tools readonly` restricts the MCP tool surface at the daemon level (not
just a client-side filter) — this agent never edits the target codebase, so
mutating tools should not even be reachable.

Then track the repo and block until the index is ready — do not guess a
timeout, do not poll in a busy loop:

```bash
gortex track <path> --wait
```

**Budget real wall-clock for this.** Indexing time scales with repo size —
roughly a second for a small repo, up to tens of minutes for a codebase in
the hundreds-of-thousands-of-LOC range, and up to ~1.5 hours observed on a
multi-million-LOC monorepo. Tell the user this up front if `gortex repos`
shows the target is not yet tracked, so they aren't left wondering why
nothing is happening.

## Step 2 — Pull structural facts (batch, not interactive)

Cost in agents that use code-graph tools tracks how much text ends up in
context, not how many queries are issued — so pull gortex's batch/file
outputs once, rather than looping many small interactive queries across the
conversation.

**Always run, regardless of daemon state — this is the reliable, correctly-
scoped backbone (verified: see "Known gortex quirks"):**

```bash
gortex analyze --kind clusters --index <path>             # run first: seeds community detection this session (see quirks above)
gortex analyze --kind suggest_boundaries --index <path>   # seed layer map from Leiden communities
gortex analyze --kind role               --index <path>   # per-symbol architectural role
gortex analyze --kind routes             --index <path>   # entry points: handler <-> route pairs
gortex analyze --kind cycles             --index <path>   # dependency cycles (risk notes)
gortex analyze --kind pagerank --limit 15 --index <path>  # most-central symbols (risk notes / hubs)
gortex analyze --kind hotspots           --index <path>   # over-coupled symbols (risk notes)
```

These six calls are enough on their own to fill every layer of Step 3's
diagram and every section of Step 4's retrodoc. Treat them as sufficient,
not as a preview of a richer source — do not hold up the diagram waiting on
`wiki` to "confirm" something `analyze` already told you.

**Only if `gortex repos` showed exactly this one tracked repo**, additionally
run, for narrative enrichment (recent activity, ownership, a rendered
community diagram you can cross-check your own diagram against):

```bash
gortex config exclude add wiki --repo <tracked-repo-name>   # do this BEFORE wiki, see quirks above
gortex wiki <path> --output <path>/wiki --no-enhance
gortex docs <path> --out <path>/docs-bundle.md
```

(`wiki`'s `--output` must be inside `<path>` — it rejects anything else.
Leave off `--enhance`: narrative synthesis is this agent's job, not
gortex's, so the two outputs don't drift out of sync with each other.)

If you ran `wiki`, copy the generated `<path>/wiki/` bundle to this agent's
own output directory (Step 6) as a supporting appendix, then leave the
original in place inside the repo (deleting it would itself trigger another
reconcile; the exclude pattern means leaving it costs nothing further). Tell
the user it's there so they can `.gitignore` it or remove it later if they
care to.

Do not invent components that aren't backed by a community, a route, or an
entry point `analyze` actually found — if the graph doesn't support a box,
the box doesn't go in the diagram.

## Step 3 — Render the diagram (fixed template — do not deviate)

Produce exactly one Mermaid `flowchart TD` with five fixed swimlanes, top to
bottom, using subgraphs. Populate each lane from Step 2's output; a lane with
nothing to put in it still appears, empty, so the reader can trust the shape
is always the same:

```
flowchart TD
    subgraph L1["External Actors"]
        direction LR
    end
    subgraph L2["Entry Points"]
        direction LR
    end
    subgraph L3["Core Domain / Service Communities"]
        direction LR
    end
    subgraph L4["Data & Persistence"]
        direction LR
    end
    subgraph L5["External Integrations"]
        direction LR
    end
    L1 --> L2 --> L3 --> L4
    L3 --> L5
```

Fixed shape conventions, applied consistently:

| Layer | Contents | Mermaid shape |
|---|---|---|
| External Actors | Human users, calling systems (from `routes`/entry points) | `([Rounded])` |
| Entry Points | HTTP/gRPC/CLI/queue-consumer handlers (from `analyze --kind routes`) | Hexagon (Mermaid double-curly node) |
| Core Domain / Service Communities | One box per top-N Leiden community (from `suggest_boundaries`), labeled with its dominant `role` | `[Rectangle]` |
| Data & Persistence | Databases, caches, queues *owned* by this repo | `[(Cylinder)]` |
| External Integrations | Third-party services/APIs called out, not owned by this repo | `[/Trapezoid/]` |

Edge rules, applied consistently:
- Draw edges between components, not inside a lane.
- The caps below are a **ceiling for busy repos, not a mandatory
  reduction**: cap at **3 edges per lane-pair** and **~25 edges total**
  only when there are genuinely more candidate edges than that to choose
  from. Verified failure mode: on a small repo with only 5 entry points
  all called by one external actor, applying the cap literally would drop
  2 of 5 real, graph-backed routes for no reason — every distinct
  component earns its own edge when the natural count is already at or
  below the cap. The cap exists to keep a repo with dozens of
  densely-connected components readable, not to trim a system that simply
  has few components.
- Never draw an edge you can't point to a specific graph fact for.

Always end the diagram with this fixed legend as a Mermaid comment block, so
every diagram this agent produces is self-describing on its own:

```
%% Legend: (rounded)=external actor, hexagon=entry point, [rect]=core module, [(cyl)]=data store, [/trap/]=external integration
%% Generated by Retrodoc - Architecture from gortex graph: <repo> @ <commit-sha>, <N> nodes / <M> edges, <date>
```

## Step 4 — Write the retrodoc (fixed outline — do not deviate)

Markdown, this exact section order, every time:

1. **Overview** — one paragraph: what the system is, primary language/
   framework mix, rough scale (file/symbol counts from Step 2's `analyze`
   output — do not use `wakeup`'s scale figures unless it was safe to run
   per Step 2, and even then cross-check them against `analyze` before
   trusting them). Load `{{ guidelines.project_context }}` first if it exists.
2. **Entry Points** — table: type, path/route, handler symbol (from
   `analyze --kind routes`).
3. **Core Modules & Responsibilities** — one subsection per Layer-3 box in
   the diagram: role classification, key symbols, approximate size.
4. **Data Flow** — narrative walk from an External Actor through the diagram
   to Data & Persistence for the 1-2 most important flows; reference the
   diagram, don't repeat it.
5. **Dependencies & Integrations** — internal cross-community edges plus
   Layer 5 externals.
6. **Risk Notes** — dependency cycles, top PageRank hubs, hotspots, from
   Step 2's `analyze` calls. Omit this section if there's nothing to
   report; don't manufacture risk to fill it.
7. **Glossary** — domain terms that recur across community/symbol names,
   only if the codebase has genuine domain vocabulary worth defining.
8. **Generation metadata** — repo path, commit SHA, gortex version
   (`gortex version`), graph size (nodes/edges), generation date, and one
   line on how this was produced (so a human reader can audit the claims
   against the underlying graph, not just trust the prose). Note explicitly
   whether `wiki`/`wakeup` contributed anything, given their scoping caveat.

## Step 5 — Fallback when gortex is unavailable

If gortex isn't installed and the user doesn't want to install it, produce
the same two fixed outputs (Steps 3-4) using `search/fileSearch`,
`search/textSearch`, and `read/readFile` instead: entry points from
routing/main files, modules from top-level directory structure, dependencies
from import statements. State explicitly, at the top of the retrodoc, that
this run is ungrounded (no code graph) and treat it as lower-confidence than
a gortex-backed run — do not present it with the same authority.

## Step 6 — Output

Write this agent's own artifacts under a directory outside the tracked repo,
e.g. `<repo-parent>/<repo-name>-retrodoc/`: `architecture.mmd` (the diagram)
and `RETRODOC.md`. If Step 2 generated a `wiki/` bundle (only possible in
single-repo-daemon mode), copy it into this same output directory as a
supporting appendix — its original, inside the repo, can stay where it is
(see Step 2). Tell the user where you put everything.
